 "final_answer": "Based on the precomputed SCAPS-1D sweep of perovskite absorber thickness (`perovskite_nip.def`):\n\n### 1. Simulated PCE Values\n- **At 200 nm:** **24.29%** ($V_{oc} = 1.400\\text{ V}$, $J_{sc} = 19.14\\text{ mA/cm}^2$, $\\text{FF} = 90.62\\%$)\n- **At 1000 nm:** **32.14%** ($V_{oc} = 1.365\\text{ V}$, $J_{sc} = 26.06\\text{ mA/cm}^2$, $\\text{FF} = 90.40\\%$)\n\n### 2. Variation of PCE with Thickness\nAcross the simulated range (200 nm to 1000 nm):\n- **Monotonic Increase with Diminishing Returns:** The simulated PCE increases monotonically from 24.29% at 200 nm to 32.14% at 1000 nm (an absolute increase of +7.85% and a relative increase of +32.33%). The steepest efficiency gains occur between 200 nm and 500 nm, after which gains taper off:\n  - 200 nm: 24.29%\n  - 300 nm: 27.88%\n  - 400 nm: 29.75% (baseline)\n  - 500 nm: 30.79%\n  - 600 nm: 31.38%\n  - 700 nm: 31.73%\n  - 800 nm: 31.95%\n  - 1000 nm: 32.14%\n- **Physical Drivers:**\n  - **$J_{sc}$ Growth:** The primary driver is a substantial increase in short-circuit current density ($J_{sc}$ rises from $19.14\\text{ mA/cm}^2$ to $26.06\\text{ mA/cm}^2$) due to enhanced optical absorption across the solar spectrum.\n  - **Slight $V_{oc}$ and FF Decline:** Open-circuit voltage ($V_{oc}$) drops marginally from $1.400\\text{ V}$ to $1.365\\text{ V}$ and fill factor ($\\text{FF}$) decreases from $90.62\\%$ to $90.40\\%$, resulting from greater bulk Shockley-Read-Hall (SRH) recombination across the thicker active layer.",
